# Line-ending policy
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# core.autocrlf is true on this machine, so git normalizes text files to LF in
# the index and expects CRLF in the worktree. AGENTS.md had been written with
# MIXED line endings (git ls-files --eol reported w/mixed while the index held
# LF). Git then normalized on read, got a different byte sequence than the
# index, and reported the file as modified forever with an EMPTY diff:
# `git diff --numstat AGENTS.md` printed nothing while `git status` still showed
# it as M. That is the worst kind of noise, because it looks like a real change
# and is not one.
#
# The fix is to state the intent explicitly rather than rely on a per-machine
# core.autocrlf. With an explicit eol= value git stops emitting the
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warning, and the file behaves the same on a
# machine with autocrlf off.
#
# SCOPE: deliberately narrow. A blanket "* text=auto" would renormalize every
# tracked file in the repo on the next checkout and bury the real diff in
# thousands of unrelated line changes. Only the file that actually exhibited the
# problem is pinned here. Add further entries as real cases appear, never as a
# blanket default.
#
# WHY -text AND NOT eol=crlf
# An earlier version of this file used `AGENTS.md text eol=crlf`, which silenced
# the warning but did NOT hold. scripts/launch-unified.mjs auto-stashes local
# changes before switching branches (line ~133, `git stash push`) and then
# checks out main (line ~145). That stash/pop round-trip rewrites the worktree
# file and restores it with MIXED line endings, so the file drifted back to
# w/mixed on the next launch and the empty-but-present modification returned.
#
# `-text` disables git's end-of-line conversion for this path entirely, so the
# bytes are stored and restored verbatim and the stash/pop cycle cannot mangle
# them. Diffing still works as text; only the conversion is off. This is the
# fix for the writer, not for the symptom.
AGENTS.md -text